          "informationUri": "https://www.glog.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "ca3324dd-1c16-3184-bdf9-a32e3576a862",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This can allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is:\n\n```cpp\nstrcat(dir,'ptrT')\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`. This function takes an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\nstrncat(dir, 'ptrT', sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1)\n```\n\nThis code will append 'ptrT' to the end of `dir`, but not more than `sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1` characters, thus ensuring that `dir` does not overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eee06281-c60a-30f0-ac7e-b2b8f467ac38",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This can allow an attacker to overwrite adjacent memory and execute arbitrary code.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is:\n\n```cpp\nstrcat(dir,'indT');\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`. Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the concatenated result plus the null terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using `strncat`:\n\n```cpp\nchar dir[SIZE];\n// ... (initialize dir)\nstrncat(dir, \"indT\", SIZE - strlen(dir) - 1);\n```\n\nIn this code, `SIZE` should be the size of the `dir` buffer. The expression `SIZE - strlen(dir) - 1` ensures that `strncat` does not write past the end of the buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strncat` and `strlen` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f79069b9-6f13-35d0-91c5-9ae00329d143",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\nIn the provided code snippet `strcat(dir,'Q')`, the function attempts to append the string 'Q' to the string `dir`. If `dir` does not have enough space to hold the additional characters, a buffer overflow occurs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat` or `strlcat`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat` or `strlcat`. Here is an example using `strncat`:\n\n```cpp\nchar dir[100]; // ensure dir has enough space\n// ... populate dir ...\nstrncat(dir, \"Q\", sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1);\n```\n\nIn this example, `sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1` calculates the remaining space in `dir`, ensuring that the string \"Q\" does not overflow `dir`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcat`, `strncat`, and `strlen` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ecbf2e14-6b66-320d-937d-877b4cb3a711",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is:\n\n```cpp\nstrcat(dir,'valT');\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`. This function takes an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\nstrncat(dir, \"valT\", sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1);\n```\n\nThis code will append the string \"valT\" to `dir`, but not more than `sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1` characters, thus ensuring that `dir` is not overflowed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcat` and `strncat` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
