              "id": "b562132e-d780-33e5-b43b-e253e44d3f00",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\nIn the provided code snippet `strcat(dir,'Q')`, the function attempts to append the string 'Q' to the string `dir`. If `dir` does not have enough space to hold the additional characters, a buffer overflow occurs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat` or `strlcat`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat` or `strlcat`. Here is an example using `strncat`:\n\n```cpp\nchar dir[100]; // ensure dir has enough space\n// ... populate dir ...\nstrncat(dir, \"Q\", sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1);\n```\n\nIn this example, `sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1` calculates the remaining space in `dir`, ensuring that the string \"Q\" does not overflow `dir`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcat`, `strncat`, and `strlen` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
