              "id": "54c501c4-9642-35a7-a353-cfb6f06fd53b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\nThe function `strcat(dir,'valA')` is particularly vulnerable because it appends the string 'valA' to the string `dir` without checking if there is enough space in `dir` to hold the additional characters.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat` or `strlcat`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to append, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat` or `strlcat` as follows:\n\n```cpp\n// Assuming dir has enough space\nstrncat(dir, \"valA\", sizeof(dir)-strlen(dir)-1);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcat` function is part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
