            {
              "id": "2b2b6bed-4f7d-3ebc-be95-848eb9a17ff4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. If an attacker can influence the data that is being concatenated, they can cause the program to crash or potentially execute arbitrary code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet `strcat(dir,'indA')`, the function `strcat` is used to append the string 'indA' to the string `dir`. If the size of `dir` is not large enough to hold the additional characters, a buffer overflow will occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nstrncat(dir, \"indA\", sizeof(dir)-strlen(dir)-1);\n```\n\nThis will append at most `sizeof(dir)-strlen(dir)-1` characters from 'indA' to `dir`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "507e8789-dfda-3288-b102-9037c7282ecf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C standard library functions that are known to be insecure. These functions are often referred to as \"banned\" functions. One such function is `sprintf`.\n\nThe `sprintf` function is used to write formatted data to a string. However, it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source data exceeds the size of the destination buffer. This can cause crashes, data corruption, and potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `sprintf` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that check the size of the destination buffer, such as `snprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `sprintf` with `snprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n// Vulnerable code\nchar buffer[50];\nsprintf(buffer, \"%s\", someString);\n\n// Fixed code\nchar buffer[50];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", someString);\n```\n\nIn the fixed code, `snprintf` checks that it does not write more than `sizeof(buffer)` characters, including the null terminator, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `sprintf` and `snprintf` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ff4d5078-5ce5-3169-a14c-139aa1bb6562",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This is because `strcat` assumes that the destination buffer has enough space to hold the source string and the null-terminating character. If the destination buffer is not large enough, `strcat` will overwrite adjacent memory, leading to undefined behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as code execution or privilege escalation.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`, `strlcat`, or `snprintf`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with a safer alternative. For example, if `dir` is a character array and `ptrA` is a string to be appended:\n\n```cpp\n// Unsafe\nstrcat(dir, 'ptrA');\n\n// Safe\nstrncpy(dir, 'ptrA', sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1);\n```\n\nIn the safe version, `strncpy` appends at most `sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1` characters from `ptrA` to `dir`, ensuring that `dir` is not overflowed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcat` and `strncpy` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0798254d-a4b3-3935-9c3e-582b151be455",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\nThe function `strcat(dir, file_indA)` is particularly vulnerable because it appends the string `file_indA` to the string `dir`. If the combined length of these strings exceeds the size of the buffer allocated for `dir`, a buffer overflow will occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `strcat` and instead use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat` or `strlcat`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to append, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat` or `strlcat` as follows:\n\n```cpp\n// Assuming dir has enough space allocated\nstrncat(dir, file_indA, sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcat`, `strncat`, `strlen` functions are part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b8876369-7117-3bbe-8300-388a11cb2d07",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. In the provided code snippet, `strcat(dir,'/')`, the function is trying to append a '/' character to the `dir` string. If the `dir` string does not have enough space to accommodate the additional character, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar dir[100]; // assuming dir is a char array of size 100\nstrncat(dir, \"/\", sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1);\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncat` is used instead of `strcat`. The third parameter to `strncat` is `sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1`, which ensures that the function does not append more characters than the `dir` string can accommodate.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strncat` and `strlen` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "20cc6ed1-5c00-3281-8694-b8d59dd03017",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\nThe function `strcat(dir, file_valA)` is particularly vulnerable because it appends the string `file_valA` to the string `dir`. If the combined length of these strings exceeds the size of the buffer allocated for `dir`, a buffer overflow will occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`, `strlcat`, or `snprintf`. These functions take an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat` or `snprintf` as follows:\n\n```cpp\n// Using strncat\nchar dir[MAX_SIZE];\nstrncat(dir, file_valA, MAX_SIZE - strlen(dir) - 1);\n\n// Using snprintf\nchar dir[MAX_SIZE];\nsnprintf(dir, MAX_SIZE, \"%s%s\", dir, file_valA);\n```\n\nIn both examples, `MAX_SIZE` is the size of the buffer allocated for `dir`. \n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strcat` function and the `<cstdio>` library for the `snprintf` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4e3e1a80-4f81-3eb7-8740-47f41881e1ee",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\nThe function `strcat(dir, file_ptrA)` is particularly vulnerable because it appends the string pointed to by `file_ptrA` to the end of the string pointed to by `dir`. If the string `dir` is not large enough to contain both strings, a buffer overflow occurs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`, `strlcat`, or `strncpy`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat` or `strncpy`. Here is an example of how to use `strncat`:\n\n```cpp\nchar dir[SIZE];\nchar *file_ptrA;\n// ...\nstrncat(dir, file_ptrA, SIZE - strlen(dir) - 1);\n```\n\nIn this example, `SIZE - strlen(dir) - 1` ensures that only enough characters are appended to `dir` to fill it up, leaving room for the null terminator.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcat` function is part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a0afa696-1091-3722-b339-c0bc4fdaa68f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. In the provided code snippet, `strcat(dir,'/')`, the function is trying to append a '/' character to the `dir` string. If the `dir` string does not have enough space to accommodate the additional character, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar dir[100]; // assuming dir is a char array of size 100\nstrncat(dir, \"/\", sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1);\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncat` is used instead of `strcat`. The third parameter to `strncat` is `sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1`, which ensures that the function does not append more characters than the `dir` string can accommodate.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strncat` and `strlen` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "70764958-1fde-3554-a2b8-f02945964fb8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C standard library functions that are known to be insecure. These functions are often referred to as \"banned\" functions. One such function is `sprintf`.\n\nThe `sprintf` function is used to write formatted data to a string. However, it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source data exceeds the size of the destination buffer. This can cause crashes, data corruption, and potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `sprintf` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that check the size of the destination buffer, such as `snprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `sprintf` with `snprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n// Vulnerable code\nchar buffer[50];\nsprintf(buffer, \"%s\", someString);\n\n// Fixed code\nchar buffer[50];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", someString);\n```\n\nIn the fixed code, `snprintf` checks that it does not write more than `sizeof(buffer)` characters, including the null terminator, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `sprintf` and `snprintf` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d40369f4-1282-3c29-80f9-6f76ccb5b546",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C standard library functions that are known to be insecure. These functions are often referred to as \"banned\" functions. One such function is `sprintf`.\n\nThe `sprintf` function is used to write formatted data to a string. However, it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source data exceeds the size of the destination buffer. This can cause crashes, data corruption, and potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `sprintf` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that check the size of the destination buffer, such as `snprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `sprintf` with `snprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n// Vulnerable code\nchar buffer[50];\nsprintf(buffer, \"%s\", someString);\n\n// Fixed code\nchar buffer[50];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", someString);\n```\n\nIn the fixed code, `snprintf` checks that it does not write more than `sizeof(buffer)` characters, including the null terminator, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `sprintf` and `snprintf` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "291945ad-b3ea-3518-b397-d665852df4dd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a memory copy operation where the source and destination buffers may overlap, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Overlapping memory regions with memcpy can result in undefined behavior, which can lead to data corruption or unpredictable program behavior. The analysis did not detect any explicit null pointer risks for either the source or destination, and the source and destination are both identified as non-null. However, the risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap remains. The source and destination are both identifiers, and the overlap is feasible due to dataflow from the destination to the source. No additional guards or capacity checks are present to mitigate this risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, replace the memory copy operation with a function that safely handles overlapping regions, such as `memmove`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping source and destination buffers safely.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(Acpy, A, sizeof(T) * nrows * ncols);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ea57d6ea-0476-39a2-ac6d-d8e3b1831642",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C standard library functions that are known to be insecure. These functions are often referred to as \"banned\" functions. One such function is `sprintf`.\n\nThe `sprintf` function is used to write formatted data to a string. However, it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source data exceeds the size of the destination buffer. This can cause crashes, data corruption, and potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `sprintf` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that check the size of the destination buffer, such as `snprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `sprintf` with `snprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n// Vulnerable code\nchar buffer[50];\nsprintf(buffer, \"%s\", someString);\n\n// Fixed code\nchar buffer[50];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", someString);\n```\n\nIn the fixed code, `snprintf` checks that it does not write more than `sizeof(buffer)` characters, including the null terminator, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `sprintf` and `snprintf` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6158b1a9-74bb-3213-a8fd-5a3ee7e3d798",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible risk of undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in a memory copy operation. The code uses a direct memory copy function, and dataflow analysis indicates that the source and destination may overlap, which is not allowed for this function and can result in unpredictable behavior. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and both the source and destination are confirmed to be non-null, so the main risk is the potential for memory region overlap. The source and destination are not string literals or C strings, so the overlap risk is not reduced by the nature of the data. This is a classic case of undefined behavior in C/C++ when using memory copy functions with potentially overlapping regions.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, replace the direct memory copy function with a function that safely handles overlapping regions. In C/C++, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy` for such cases. `memmove` is designed to correctly handle overlapping source and destination buffers.\n\n**Remediation Example:**\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(this->data(), that.data(), sizeof(T) * this->m_nmemb);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that even if the source and destination memory regions overlap, the copy will be performed safely without causing undefined behavior.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "74106162-98f4-36b9-ad57-edfdc67ddda6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions in a call to a memory copy function. The destination and source expressions are derived from similar indexed accesses, and dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may overlap. The classification explicitly states a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap, and the verdict is marked as genuine. Additionally, the source may be null, which further increases the risk. There are no explicit guards or evidence that the memory regions are guaranteed to be non-overlapping. This type of issue can lead to unpredictable program behavior, data corruption, or crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use a function that is safe for overlapping memory, such as `memmove`, instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` correctly handles overlapping source and destination regions, ensuring data is copied safely.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove((*this)[batch_index], that[batch_index], num_bytes);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
